  "query_preview": "period of time. Locations along the coast will see the \nbiggest impacts as coastal flooding will be likely due to \npersistent strong onshore flow (see Key Message 3 below). It \nwon't be good beach weather either as large waves and strong \nwinds will lead to elevated rip current risks, as well as the \nrisk for potential beach erosion. Model differences and timing\nlimitations are still creating some uncertainty in the details,\nso please stay tuned to the latest forecast as we work out the \ndetails. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Widespread minor to moderate tidal flooding \n(inundation of 1-2 feet AGL in vulnerable areas) is possible by the \nmiddle of the week due to strong northeast winds.\n\nStrong northeast winds are expected from Tuesday night through at \nleast Thursday, which will cause water levels to rise across much of \nthe area (especially along the bay, tidal rivers, and Atlantic \ncoast). No tidal flooding is expected through Tuesday. A significant \nrise in tidal anomalies is forecast on Wednesday. Minor tidal \nflooding is possible as early as the Wednesday morning high tide \ncycle, with minor to moderate flooding forecast during the Wednesday \nevening high tide. This corresponds to inundation of 1-2 ft AGL in \nvulnerable areas. Specifics will become more clear as we get closer. \nThe highest water levels will likely occur along the south/west side \nof the Ches Bay, tidal York/James, and Atlantic coast. The minor to \nmoderate tidal flooding potentially persists through several tide \ncycles as NE winds remain elevated.",
